% ReportKit theme: executive -- EXPERIMENTAL shell, Phase B of the
% multi-format publication architecture spec (decisions D8, D9). "Do not
% release between B and C" (D8): this file exists so the slides renderer and
% presentation composition API (reportkit-presentation.sty) have a real
% theme to compile against, not to be a finished visual design. Phase C
% ("Executive theme") tunes the actual assertion-evidence hierarchy, grid,
% density, and chart/table/diagram styles per the renderer spec, and
% promotes this theme from experimental to stable only after visual review
% (see publications.py's THEMES["executive"] stability field).
%
% Owns fonts, palette, and renderer-neutral semantic appearance -- the same
% split A4/D11 established for default and institutional-research (compare
% reportkit-theme-default.sty's own header comment). Geometry, running
% furniture, and other slides-specific values live in
% reportkit-theme-executive-slides.sty, this theme's only adapter so far
% (Phase C may add reportkit-theme-executive-paged.sty; D11's two-layer load
% means that would not require changing this file).
\NeedsTeXFormat{LaTeX2e}
\ProvidesPackage{reportkit-theme-executive}[2026/09/13 v0.1.0 ReportKit executive theme (experimental)]

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Engine guard (decision D9: new visual themes require LuaLaTeX)
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Same defense-in-depth pattern reportkit-theme-institutional-research.sty
% uses: config.py's theme_engine_conflict() already refuses to build this
% theme under any other engine before a compiler runs; this is the same rule
% enforced a second time at the TeX level.
\RequirePackage{iftex}
\ifPDFTeX
  \PackageError{reportkit-theme-executive}%
    {The executive theme requires LuaLaTeX}%
    {Compile with lualatex, or set document.engine: lualatex in publication.yaml.}
\fi

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Fonts -- reuses the bundled Libertinus assets already staged for the
% default/longform fixtures (font_data/reportkit-libertinus-fonts.tar.gz via
% the toolchain, or ambient system fonts). D9 recommends Google Sans (already
% bundled for institutional-research) for executive/venture; this shell uses
% Libertinus instead, deliberately, because it needs no font_path/font_policy
% plumbing to compile in any LuaLaTeX environment this repository already
% supports, and this file's own header says explicitly not to treat its
% visual choices as final. Phase C's design review picks the real family.
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\RequirePackage{libertinus}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Palette. Distinct hex values from both existing themes' palettes (not
% reused, not tuned -- placeholders a design review replaces in Phase C).
% Accent is this theme's required semantic role for
% reportkit-presentation.sty's kicker/label color, the same role name
% reportkit-theme-institutional-research.sty already established.
% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
